<p>Water damage claims in San Antonio are among the most common, and the most misunderstood, property insurance claims. Water can come from a burst supply line, a failed water heater in the garage or attic, an overflowing dishwasher, a roof opened by hail, or pipes that split during a freeze like Winter Storm Uri in February 2021. Whether the claim is covered usually depends less on how much damage there is and more on where the water came from, how suddenly it happened, and how long it went on before anyone noticed.</p>
<p>This page explains how insurers sort water losses, the exclusions that come up most often, and how a public adjuster in San Antonio may help. If the damage came from a hard freeze, see our <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a> page as well.</p>

<h2>Sudden and Accidental vs. Gradual Damage</h2>
<p>Most homeowners and commercial policies cover water damage that is <strong>sudden and accidental</strong>, such as a supply line that bursts or a water heater tank that ruptures. Most exclude damage from <strong>continuous or repeated seepage or leakage</strong> over weeks or months, along with wear and tear, deterioration, and lack of maintenance. That single distinction drives many water damage denials.</p>
