<tr><td>Rising water or surface runoff</td><td>Not under standard policies</td><td>Requires flood insurance</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The gray area is a hidden leak, for example a pinhole leak inside a wall that went unnoticed until the drywall softened. Some policies treat hidden leaks differently from visible ones the owner ignored. The wording of your policy and the evidence about timing both matter. If water entered from outside during heavy rain, the question shifts to whether it came through a storm-created opening or rose from the ground; see <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Common Water Losses Around San Antonio</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Attic plumbing and equipment.</strong> Many local homes have water heaters, air handlers with condensate lines, and supply lines in the attic. A failure there sends water through ceilings and insulation.</li>
<li><strong>Storm-related roof leaks.</strong> Hail and wind can open roofs during spring storms. See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Freeze breaks.</strong> Pipes in attics and exterior walls are vulnerable during hard freezes.</li>
<li><strong>Slab leaks.</strong> Most homes here sit on slab-on-grade foundations over expansive clay soils. A leak under the slab can damage flooring and walls, but foundation movement and settling are commonly excluded.</li>
<li><strong>Appliance supply lines.</strong> Washing machine, refrigerator, and dishwasher lines can fail without warning.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial losses.</strong> Fire sprinkler discharges, rooftop unit leaks, and plumbing failures in multi-tenant buildings. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>.</li>
</ul>

