<h2>Water From the Utility Side</h2>
<p>Most water damage claims involve plumbing on the owner's side of the meter. If you believe water came from a public main or from equipment on the utility side, contact SAWS about that issue directly; any process the utility has is separate from your property insurance claim. Your own policy still governs damage to your property, so report the loss to your insurer as well. A sudden jump on your SAWS bill can also help show when a leak on your side began. General information is available from <a href="https://www.saws.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SAWS</a>.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Mold: Know the Limits</h2>
<p>Mold is where many water claims run into trouble. Many Texas policies exclude mold or cap coverage at a set dollar amount, and some cover remediation only when mold results directly from a covered water loss. Mold that grew because of a long-term leak, or because drying was delayed, is often excluded. A public adjuster cannot make a mold exclusion disappear. What helps is prompt action: stop the water, start professional drying quickly, keep moisture readings, and document that you acted reasonably. If mold has already developed, separate the cost of mold remediation from the cost of the underlying water damage repairs, because they may be treated very differently under your policy.</p>

<h2>Deductibles, Depreciation, and Limits</h2>
<p>Water losses usually fall under your policy's all-other-perils deductible rather than the wind/hail deductible, unless the water came in through storm damage. Flooring, cabinets, and contents may be depreciated, with replacement cost released after repairs if your policy provides it. Some policies also set sublimits for specific water-related items. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> shows how a deductible and depreciation affect what an insurer pays.</p>

<h2>Documenting a Water Damage Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li>Stop the water at the shut-off valve and photograph the source before repairs.</li>
<li>Keep the failed part (supply line, valve, or pipe section) if a plumber removes it.</li>
<li>Get a written plumber's report describing the cause of the failure.</li>
<li>Hire a mitigation company promptly and keep moisture maps, photos, and drying logs.</li>
<li>Photograph every affected room, including cabinets, baseboards, and flooring.</li>
<li>List damaged contents with age and approximate cost.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through these steps in more detail.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster Can Help With Water Damage</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster represents policyholders on water damage claims. We may review your policy's water, seepage, mold, and access provisions, document the loss room by room, prepare a line-item estimate in Xactimate that includes access, tear-out, drying, and rebuild, and respond to insurer arguments about gradual leakage or wear and tear. If a water claim was denied, see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>. If the claim was paid but the estimate left items out, we can review it for an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a>.</p>
<p>We cannot guarantee a result; coverage depends on your policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. For legal questions about deadlines or disputes, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
